<article class="env-lemma" id="0198">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0198">Lemma <span data-tag="0198">14.23.4</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{A}$ be an abelian category. Let $U$ be a simplicial object of $\mathcal{A}$. The canonical map $N(U_ n) \to U_ n$ gives rise to a morphism of complexes $N(U) \to s(U)$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This is clear because the differential on $s(U)_ n = U_ n$ is $\sum (-1)^ i d^ n_ i$ and the maps $d^ n_ i$, $i < n$ are zero on $N(U_ n)$, whereas the restriction of $(-1)^ nd^ n_ n$ is the boundary map of $N(U)$ by definition. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>